---
import SourceCode from '../components/SourceCode.astro';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { adapterSource, coreSource, decisions, rapierSource, troikaSource } from '../lib/links';
import { sitePath } from '../lib/site-path';
import {
  agentSnippet,
  autoRotate,
  entry,
  installation,
  labelSnippet,
  lending,
  physicsSnippet,
  renderers,
  runtime,
  sharing,
  teaser,
  testing,
  transitions,
  view,
  xrSnippet,
} from '../lib/snippets';
---
<Layout title="Guide — effect-three">
  <section class="page-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">A small beginning</p>
    <h1>From Model to mesh.</h1>
    <p>effect-three turns plain scene descriptions into Three.js objects, keeps them in step with your Model, and lets Effect own every lifetime.</p>
  </section>
  <div class="docs-layout">
    <nav class="docs-nav" aria-label="On this page">
      <a href="#installation">Installation</a><a href="#first-scene">Your first scene</a><a href="#foldkit">Using Foldkit</a><a href="#identity">Keys and identity</a><a href="#resources">Resources</a><a href="#animation">Animation</a><a href="#controls">Controls</a><a href="#physics">Physics</a><a href="#text">Text and labels</a><a href="#staging">Staging</a><a href="#renderers">Renderers</a><a href="#xr">XR</a><a href="#runtime">Without Foldkit</a><a href="#testing">Testing</a><a href="#agents">Agents</a><a href="#architecture">Architecture</a>
    </nav>
    <article class="docs-content">
      <section id="installation">
        <h2>Install the packages.</h2>
        <p>The core works anywhere Effect does. The Foldkit adapter adds the browser pieces: the renderers (WebGL, or WebGPU with <code>webgpuRendererFactory</code>), the animation-frame loop and the scene element.</p>
        <div class="code-panel"><SourceCode code={installation} lang="sh" sourceId="installation.sh" /></div>
        <p>Add <code>@opsydyn/effect-three-rapier</code> for physics and <code>@opsydyn/effect-three-troika</code> for labels when a scene needs them. The four packages are released together at one version, and the adapters take the core as a peer at exactly that version, so install matching versions. They are <code>0.x</code>: the public surface is what the API reports in each package's <code>etc/</code> record, and a change that breaks it bumps the minor version.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="first-scene">
        <h2>Describe a scene.</h2>
        <p>A scene is plain data: a camera, shared resources and keyed nodes. Constructors fill in defaults, so a description stays short.</p>
        <div class="code-panel"><SourceCode code={teaser} lang="ts" sourceId="scene.ts" /></div>
        <p>Rotation belongs either to the Model (<code>fixed</code>) or to a behaviour (<code>spin</code>), never both. Behaviours are pure functions of scene time, so frames are deterministic.</p>
        <p>Meshes are built from <code>box</code>, <code>sphere</code>, <code>plane</code>, <code>cylinder</code>, <code>cone</code>, <code>capsule</code>, <code>torus</code>, <code>ring</code>, <code>circle</code> or <code>text</code>, with Drei's <code>roundedBox</code> and <code>backdrop</code> for product shots; <code>line</code> draws Drei's fat lines, pixels or scene units wide and dashed if you like, through points of your own or along a curve (<code>catmullRomPoints</code>, <code>cubicBezierPoints</code>, <code>quadraticBezierPoints</code>), and outlines a mesh's edges with <code>{"edges: edges()"}</code>; <code>points</code>, <code>sparkles</code> and <code>stars</code> are Drei's, seeded so they are the same every time, and <code>trail()</code> leaves a ribbon behind anything that moves; <code>instances</code> draws thousands of copies of one in a single call. A group with <code>{"detail: detailed({ distances: [0, 10, 20] })"}</code> shows one child at a time by the camera's distance, as Drei's <code>Detailed</code>.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="foldkit">
        <h2>Render it with Foldkit.</h2>
        <p>Create one environment for the application, then render scenes from your view. Pointer interaction comes back as your own typed Messages: clicks, double-clicks, context menus, hover and movement, the wheel, and misses for clearing a selection. Only the handlers you pass are bound.</p>
        <div class="code-panel"><SourceCode code={entry} lang="ts" sourceId="entry.ts" /></div>
        <div class="code-panel"><SourceCode code={view} lang="ts" sourceId="view.ts" /></div>
        <p>Unmounting the element releases its renderer, WebGL context, GPU resources, listeners and frame subscription. While Foldkit DevTools time-travels, the scene shows the historical state and ignores pointer input.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href={sitePath('/examples/playground/')}>Try the playground <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </section>
      <section id="identity">
        <h2>Keys keep objects alive.</h2>
        <p>Keys are unique across the whole scene. A node keeps its Three.js object while its key and kind stay the same, including when it moves to another group. A structurally equal scene costs no host changes at all, which matters because Foldkit sends a fresh scene on every render.</p>
        <p>Updating 1,000 meshes takes under 100 µs, whether nothing changed or one property did; then exactly one operation is applied.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="resources">
        <h2>Share what the GPU holds.</h2>
        <p>Declare a geometry or material once in <code>resources</code>, then refer to it by id. Shared resources are reference-counted across every scene on the page and disposed when their last user goes. Owned resources live and die with their mesh.</p>
        <div class="code-panel"><SourceCode code={sharing} lang="ts" sourceId="shared.ts" /></div>
        <p>What the application owns, it lends: loaded models, textures and geometries go to the store under an id, scenes show them wherever a description names that id (even before it is lent), and nothing the application lends is ever disposed by the scene.</p>
        <div class="code-panel"><SourceCode code={lending} lang="ts" sourceId="lending.ts" /></div>
        <a class="text-link" href={sitePath('/examples/shared/')}>Try shared resources <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </section>
      <section id="animation">
        <h2>Animate without update.</h2>
        <p>Behaviours run in the frame loop, outside Foldkit's update, so a spinning scene never causes a Model update. Scenes pause their frames when off screen.</p>
        <p>Transitions ease <code>position</code> and <code>scale</code> to whatever the Model says next. A new target mid-flight starts from the pose on screen, and a node moving to another group keeps its place in the world while it travels.</p>
        <div class="code-panel"><SourceCode code={transitions} lang="ts" sourceId="transitions.ts" /></div>
        <p><code>rotation: billboard()</code> keeps a node facing the camera, as Drei's <code>Billboard</code>, and a camera's <code>{"shake: cameraShake({ decay: true })"}</code> shakes it once, as Drei's <code>CameraShake</code>: seeded noise, so the same moment shakes the same way.</p>
        <p>Under <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>, behaviours freeze, shakes hold still and transitions land at once while frames keep rendering.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href={sitePath('/examples/playground/')}>Watch cubes glide between shelves <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </section>
      <section id="controls">
        <h2>Keep the camera in the Model.</h2>
        <p>With <code>{"scene({ gestures: 'scene' })"}</code> the scene claims drags, the wheel and pinches, and reports each as a <code>Gesture</code> Message. <code>orbit(model.camera, gesture)</code> returns the next camera, as three.js's <code>OrbitControls</code> would move it, zooming toward the pointer if you ask. <code>orbitKey</code> handles arrow keys, and <code>fitCamera</code> frames a box the scene measured for you, as Drei's <code>Bounds</code> does.</p>
        <p>A camera's <code>damping</code> eases the rendered camera to the Model's, views included. <code>autoRotate</code> turns it round its target by itself until someone touches it; before that gesture, an <code>AutoRotated</code> Message says where it got to, so the Model keeps it and the orbit carries on from there.</p>
        <div class="code-panel"><SourceCode code={autoRotate} lang="ts" sourceId="camera.ts" /></div>
        <p>Objects move the same way. A transform gizmo is three's <code>TransformControls</code> as data: <code>gizmoNodes</code> draws its arrows, planes and rings from the Model, and <code>pressGizmo</code> and <code>dragGizmo</code> turn the press and drag rays into the next placement, as three's controls would, snaps and local axes included. The Foldkit adapter's <code>TransformGizmo</code> wraps it as a submodel that reports <code>Moved</code> and <code>Finished</code>, so the page keeps every placement and undo is a list. Box select is three's <code>SelectionBox</code> the same way: <code>boxSelect</code> returns the keys inside a box on the canvas, and the adapter's <code>BoxSelect</code> draws it from shift-drags, ending on the scene's <code>GestureEnd</code>. The axes in the corner are Drei's <code>GizmoHelper</code>: <code>viewportGizmoNodes</code> in the scene's <code>hud</code>, which draws nodes over everything with a camera of its own, and <code>cameraAlong</code> turns the camera to look from the axis pressed. Drei's <code>PivotControls</code> are there too, every handle at once: <code>pivotNodes</code>, <code>pressPivot</code> and <code>dragPivot</code>, and the adapter's <code>PivotControls</code>. A measuring tape is built the same way: <code>placeTapeEnd</code> and <code>pointTape</code> return the next tape, and the adapter's <code>MeasuringTape</code> labels its length.</p>
        <p>Because a scene is data with a schema, the tools an editor needs come from it. <code>SceneFile.save</code> and <code>SceneFile.read</code> write and read scenes as JSON in a versioned envelope. <code>History</code> keeps every Model value as a tree, so undoing and then changing something starts a branch instead of losing what you undid. <code>sceneChanges</code> says what changed between two scenes, line by line, for review. <code>Inspector.fields</code> reads any value's fields from its schema, and <code>Inspector.edit</code> checks each change with that schema, so the adapter's <code>inspectorView</code> is a form that can never put an invalid value in your Model.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href={sitePath('/examples/studio/')}>Orbit the studio <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        <a class="text-link" href={sitePath('/examples/arrange/')}>Move, turn and scale <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </section>
      <section id="physics">
        <h2>Describe bodies; let Rapier move them.</h2>
        <p>Give a mesh, group, loaded model or instances node a <code>body()</code> under <code>{"scene({ physics: physics() })"}</code>, and pass <code>loadRapier()</code> from <code>@opsydyn/effect-three-rapier</code> to the environment. Colliders come from geometry. Kicks are impulses with an id, applied once. Joints (fixed, spherical, revolute, prismatic, rope, spring, with motors) are data in <code>{"physics({ joints })"}</code>. Give a body colliders of its own when its geometry is the wrong shape. Collisions and contact forces return as Messages naming the node, the instance when there is one, and the collider's <code>name</code> as its part; <code>{"physics({ debug: true })"}</code> draws every collider.</p>
        <p>Collision groups (<code>interactionGroups</code>) decide which bodies meet, sensors report bodies entering and leaving without stopping them, and <code>contactForces</code> sets the threshold above which a body reports how hard it was pushed.</p>
        <div class="code-panel"><SourceCode code={physicsSnippet} lang="ts" sourceId="machine.ts" /></div>
        <a class="text-link" href={sitePath('/examples/toybox/')}>Throw the toys around <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        <a class="text-link" href={sitePath('/examples/marbles/')}>Sort marbles by colour <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        <a class="text-link" href={sitePath('/examples/bowling/')}>Bowl a frame <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </section>
      <section id="text">
        <h2>Set text two ways.</h2>
        <p><code>text()</code> is geometry from a typeface font, as Drei's <code>Text3D</code>: extruded or flat, with letter spacing, line height and alignment. <code>label()</code> is crisp distance-field text at any size, as Drei's <code>Text</code>, drawn by troika once you pass <code>troikaLabels()</code> from <code>@opsydyn/effect-three-troika</code> (or its <code>/webgpu</code> entry, which draws under WebGPU too). Labels wrap and take anchors and outlines; give them a TTF, OTF or WOFF font.</p>
        <p>Colour runs (<code>colorRanges</code>), curves (<code>curveRadius</code>) and a scene <code>material</code> to light the glyphs are fields too. A label named in <code>{"scene({ measure })"}</code> reports its laid-out size as <code>LabelMeasured</code>, so the Model can size what goes behind it.</p>
        <div class="code-panel"><SourceCode code={labelSnippet} lang="ts" sourceId="label.ts" /></div>
        <a class="text-link" href={sitePath('/examples/workshop/')}>See both in the workshop <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        <a class="text-link" href={sitePath('/examples/gallery/')}>Walk the type gallery <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </section>
      <section id="staging">
        <h2>Light and stage it.</h2>
        <p><code>environment()</code>, <code>sky()</code> and <code>contactShadows()</code> are Drei's staging as scene fields, and <code>shaderGrid()</code> is its fading grid. <code>stage()</code> returns Drei's <code>Stage</code> lighting to spread into a scene. Centre content with <code>centerOffset</code> on a measured box, and load one of Drei's environment presets with <code>environmentPresetUrl</code>.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href={sitePath('/examples/playground/')}>Stand on the fading grid <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </section>
      <section id="renderers">
        <h2>WebGL or WebGPU.</h2>
        <p>The default renderer is WebGL 2. <code>webgpuRendererFactory</code> uses three's <code>WebGPURenderer</code>: WebGPU where the browser has it, its WebGL 2 backend otherwise. Each renderer brings a render kit, so skies, shader grids, contact shadows and post-processing have WebGL and TSL versions, and the same scene draws on either.</p>
        <div class="code-panel"><SourceCode code={renderers} lang="ts" sourceId="renderer.ts" /></div>
        <p>Labels draw under WebGPU too: take <code>troikaLabels</code> from <code>@opsydyn/effect-three-troika/webgpu</code>. troika still lays the text out and builds its glyphs, and a node material draws them, outlines, curves, colour ranges and lit materials included. Known limits: WebXR on <code>WebGPURenderer</code> is wired but untested, and the DOM overlay is checked only in the session request. Every example takes <code>?renderer=webgpu</code> in its address.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="xr">
        <h2>Step into it.</h2>
        <p>A scene with <code>{"xr({ origin, heading })"}</code> can be presented in WebXR. Enter and exit with <code>environment.xr</code> Effects from your Commands. Controllers and hands hover, click and drag along their rays. Thumbsticks and buttons arrive as Messages: <code>locomote</code> turns and steps the player by returning new <code>xr</code> settings for your Model. In AR, <code>{"xr({ hitTest: true })"}</code> reports the real surfaces a select points at. <code>{"xr({ glide })"}</code> moves the player with the sticks inside the session, and <code>XrMoved</code> says where they stopped.</p>
        <p>Groups can ride tracked things with <code>follow</code>: the head, a hand joint, a controller, an anchor the scene placed with <code>{"xr({ anchors })"}</code>, or a plane or mesh the session detected (<code>XrPlanes</code> and <code>XrMeshes</code> Messages, with <code>planes: true</code> and <code>meshes: true</code>). A detected mesh's geometry is lent to the scene, so <code>xrMesh(id)</code> draws it with any material. <code>{"xr({ domOverlay: true })"}</code> keeps HTML overlays in AR.</p>
        <div class="code-panel"><SourceCode code={xrSnippet} lang="ts" sourceId="room.ts" /></div>
        <a class="text-link" href={sitePath('/examples/toybox/')}>Enter VR in the toybox <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        <a class="text-link" href={sitePath('/examples/room/')}>Step into the room <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </section>
      <section id="runtime">
        <h2>Use the runtime directly.</h2>
        <p>Outside Foldkit, start a scene inside a Scope. Startup is atomic: if the description is invalid, the renderer can't be created, or startup is interrupted, nothing is left behind.</p>
        <div class="code-panel"><SourceCode code={runtime} lang="ts" sourceId="runtime.ts" /></div>
      </section>
      <section id="testing">
        <h2>Test it headless.</h2>
        <p>A recording renderer, a manual frame scheduler and Effect's TestClock make scene tests deterministic, with no browser and no GPU.</p>
        <div class="code-panel"><SourceCode code={testing} lang="ts" sourceId="scene.test.ts" /></div>
      </section>
      <section id="agents">
        <h2>Let an agent arrange it.</h2>
        <p>Every change to a scene is a Schema-typed Message, so an assistant can make changes through the same door a click does. Export <code>devTools</code> with your <code>Message</code> schema, add <code>@foldkit/devtools-mcp</code> to your agent, and in development it can read the Model, list what happened, and dispatch Messages. Each one is decoded first: a malformed one is refused, and a valid one goes through <code>update</code> like any other, so it shows in DevTools history, where time travel can step back past it. With several pages open, name the page with <code>runtime_id</code>; in a monorepo, point <code>FOLDKIT_PROJECT_ROOT</code> at the app.</p>
        <p>Give an assistant words rather than numbers. <code>describeScene</code> and <code>formatSummary</code> say what a scene holds in a few lines; <code>shotCamera</code> frames things from a named angle and <code>moodStaging</code> lights a scene by mood. The editor example offers them as Messages, <code>PlacedThing</code>, <code>FramedShot</code> and <code>ChoseMood</code>, that an agent dispatches like any click.</p>
        <p>Peers that run the same <code>update</code> stay in the same state if they apply the same inputs in the same order. <code>Lockstep</code> is that protocol as data: one packet per peer per turn, a turn applied when all are in, newcomers admitted in a turn and welcomed with the state. Bring any transport; the editor example uses a <code>BroadcastChannel</code>, so two tabs edit one scene with one shared history.</p>
        <div class="code-panel"><SourceCode code={agentSnippet} lang="ts" sourceId="room/main.ts" /></div>
        <a class="text-link" href={sitePath('/examples/room/')}>Furnish the room <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </section>
      <section id="architecture">
        <h2>How it fits together.</h2>
        <div class="module-grid">
          {[
            [coreSource + '/src/kernel', 'Kernel', 'Plain TypeScript: descriptions, keyed diff, applier, resources and behaviours. It never imports Effect.', 'effect-three/kernel'],
            [coreSource + '/src/runtime', 'Runtime', 'Effect services, scopes and typed errors around the kernel.', 'effect-three'],
            [adapterSource, 'Foldkit adapter', 'The scene element, WebGL and WebGPU renderers, frame loop, assets, XR sessions and DOM bridge.', 'effect-three-foldkit'],
            [rapierSource, 'Rapier physics', 'The physics engine: bodies, colliders and joints described in scenes, simulated by Rapier.', 'effect-three-rapier'],
            [troikaSource, 'Troika labels', 'The label engine: distance-field text drawn by troika, with colour runs, curves, materials and measured layouts.', 'effect-three-troika'],
            [decisions, 'Decisions', 'The plan, architecture decision records and benchmark results.', 'docs/effect-three'],
          ].map(([href, name, description, path]) => <a href={href}>
            <h3>{name} <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></h3><p>{description}</p><code>{path}</code>
          </a>)}
        </div>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>
